## Start here

- People: this file.
- AI agents: [AGENTS.md](AGENTS.md). `CLAUDE.md` imports it for Claude Code.
- Setting up the code: run `/afe-init` in Claude Code. It asks the owner what to build and with
  which stack, scaffolds the code, wires the checks, and writes down how to run the product.

## Checks

```bash
scripts/check.sh
```

The full check runs the project's code checks (`scripts/checks.d/`), the link check, and the
template integrity. `--verbose` prints everything. CI runs the same check in the `check`
workflow.

Requires Git, bash, and Node.js 20 or later; nothing else to install. External links are not
checked; a project that wants it adds a check such as [lychee](https://lychee.cli.rs/) in
`scripts/checks.d/`.

## What an agent may not do

Hooks and permission rules hold these whatever the agent decides: no push to the default
branch, no forced push, no reading of `.env` files or keys (`.env.example` is readable), a
prompt before a merge, a release, a publish, or a change of repository settings, and a prompt
before a known secret format is written. `.claude/risky-paths` lists the paths where a mistake
costs most; the project edits it.

How an agent works on a task (questions, plan, review, pull request) is not part of this
repository: it comes from the developer's own setup.

## Sandbox

The [Claude Code sandbox](https://code.claude.com/docs/en/sandboxing) is off unless the owner
turns it on in `/afe-init`, which writes its `sandbox` block into `.claude/settings.project.json`
and regenerates `.claude/settings.json`. It confines the shell commands Claude runs to writes in
the working and temporary directories and to allowed network hosts, on macOS, Linux, and WSL2.
It does not cover Claude's file and web tools, the hooks, MCP servers, or the commands it
excludes, which run with full access; Docker does not run inside it, and commands that write
`.claude/`, such as a template update, run outside it.

## Modules

The answers in `.afe-answers.json` turn these on or off:

- `code_review` (default off): Claude reviews each pull request that changes code in CI, once,
  when it opens or leaves draft. It needs the Claude GitHub App and the
  `CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN` secret.
- `secret_scan` (default on): the `secret-scan` workflow scans pull requests and pushes to `main`
  for leaked secrets.

## Protected main branch

Changes reach `main` only through pull requests with a passing `check`, merged with a merge
commit. After the first push, a repository admin applies the repository settings and the GitHub
ruleset once; `--dry-run` shows them first, and the header of the script says what they are.
Rulesets need a public repository or, for a private one, a paid plan. Without the ruleset,
nothing on the server stops the merge of a commit whose checks failed or never ran: that the
owner merges is then the only control.

```bash
scripts/protect-branch.sh
```

